Abstract

The interaction between string theory and condensed matter physics has historically been asymmetric. String-theoretic tools—particularly holography arising from the correspondence between anti-de Sitter gravity and conformal field theories—have been extensively applied to strongly correlated condensed matter systems. Holographic superconductors, strange metals, non-Fermi liquids, and quantum critical phases all emerged from this program.

However, the reverse direction has remained largely unexplored. Condensed matter physics has developed entirely new notions of organization beyond symmetry breaking: topological order, anyonic excitations, string-net condensates, higher-form symmetries, subsystem symmetries, fracton phases, quantum error-correcting codes, and tensor-network geometries. These structures may contain clues regarding the microscopic organization of spacetime itself.

This paper proposes a new research framework in which condensed matter phases are treated not as systems to be modeled by string theory but as sources of fundamental insight capable of generating novel string dynamics, new vacuum sectors, alternative compactifications, emergent dimensions, and non-geometric backgrounds.

We develop a program in which string vacua become phases of quantum order, extra dimensions emerge from entanglement structure, D-branes become defects in topological media, and fracton behavior provides a mechanism for localization of matter and stabilization of compactifications.

The resulting framework suggests that spacetime may resemble a condensed phase of information whose microscopic organization is closer to topological quantum matter than to classical geometry.

