Conclusion

This white paper proposes a new research direction in which condensed matter physics is elevated from an application domain of string theory to a source of foundational principles. By importing topological order, anyons, string-net condensates, fracton phases, tensor-network geometry, and quantum error-correcting structures into the core of string-theoretic thinking, we obtain a radically different picture of fundamental physics.

In this framework, string vacua are not geometric backgrounds but quantum phases; extra dimensions are emergent entanglement structures; branes are topological defects; and spacetime itself is a condensed phase of quantum information. The long-standing landscape problem becomes a classification problem of stable quantum orders, potentially replacing anthropic reasoning with dynamical phase selection.

The central hypothesis is simple but far-reaching: the deepest clues about quantum gravity may not come from applying string theory to condensed matter systems, but from allowing condensed matter’s most exotic phases to redefine what string theory itself can be.
